**W1-D4-Revision-Class_Methods_Employee**

---

```
KEY POINTS:
- class variable = shared by ALL objects, defined at top of class
- instance variable = belongs to one object, defined with self.x in __init__
- Employee.total_employees = correct way to update class variable
- self.total_employees = WRONG — creates separate copy per object
- @classmethod = works on class itself, takes cls not self
- cls = the class itself (Employee), not one specific object
- classmethod callable on class directly: Employee.get_total()
- from_string() = alternate way to create object from different input format
- Django Post.objects.all/create/filter = same classmethod pattern
```

---

```python
# QUICK REFERENCE CODE

class Employee:
    total_employees = 0       # class variable — shared by all
    company_name = "TechCorp" # class variable — same for all

    def __init__(self, name, salary):
        self.name = name      # instance variable — separate per object
        self.salary = salary
        Employee.total_employees += 1  # update class variable correctly

    @classmethod
    def get_total_employees(cls):
        return cls.total_employees  # cls = Employee class itself

    @classmethod
    def from_string(cls, data_string):
        name, salary = data_string.split("-")  # "Ravi-30000" → ["Ravi","30000"]
        return cls(name, int(salary))          # creates new Employee object


e1 = Employee("Ravi", 30000)
e2 = Employee("Sai", 40000)
e3 = Employee.from_string("Kiran-28000")

print(Employee.get_total_employees())  # 3
print(Employee.company_name)           # TechCorp
print(e3.name, e3.salary)             # Kiran 28000
```

